## The one thing to decide

**Hall of Fame inducts people (individuals or teams of members), not programs**, and its criteria require *"sustained excellence over a long period."* "Lifesaving CPR" is a 2024-2025 program, so as written it does not fit either test.

My recommendation: put the **Lifesaving CPR initiative forward for the SLSA Innovation Award** (purpose-built for exactly this, no long-period bar) as first preference. If you want the Hall of Fame specifically, nominate the **Lifesaving CPR project team** under the Surf Lifesaving category, accepting the long-period criterion as a known weakness. Full reasoning in file 02.

## How the two nominations connect

Chris Jacobson **introducing Lifesaving CPR** is the marquee proof point for his Life Membership case: the interpretive guidelines literally cite *"the introduction of CPR"* as the kind of distinguished contribution that qualifies, against which *"a patrol captain for 20 years would not of itself qualify."* So keep the two cases separate but mutually reinforcing: Chris's individual distinguished-service case (Life Membership), and the initiative's case (Innovation Award or team Hall of Fame). Do not run Chris individually for the Hall of Fame as well; it would dilute both.

## Key dates and rules

- **Life Membership (SLSA Honours):** online Jotform only; deadlines **25 January** and **18 July**. Must be nominated by the President or two (2) SLSA members. Re-nomination allowed after 2 years if unsuccessful.
- **Hall of Fame:** club-endorsed, forwarded to **honours@slsa.asn.au**; considered twice yearly. Re-submission after 2 years if not endorsed.

## Consolidated "you supply" list

**Chris Jacobson (Life Membership):** Member ID · current club · DOB · address · mobile · email · proposer & seconder (or President) · photo · Surfguard printout · Records of Service docs (drafts provided, convert to PDF) · letters of recommendation. Confirm: club names, club-vs-state award attributions, branch-vs-state event roles.

